    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test module; 1/1 passing, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented per-country national-number length validation for the E.164 telephone on `POST /api/owners`, rejecting with 400 when the national length is wrong for the country code.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`E164Telephone.java`** \u2014 added `validateNationalNumberLength(String e164)`: after the E.164 form is built, it checks the national digits against the country code \u2014 `+61` \u2192 exactly 9, `+1` \u2192 exactly 10 \u2014 throwing `InvalidTelephoneException` on mismatch. Other country codes keep only the existing generic 8\u201315 digit bound. I deliberately left `normalize`/`normalizeOrNull` lenient so duplicate-detection over legacy/seed owners (e.g. the seed `6085551023` numbers) is unaffected.\n\n2. **`ValidateTelephoneCountryLength.java`** (new function step) \u2014 reads the already-normalized `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` and calls the validator.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step in right after `normalizeTelephone` (E.164 is formed by then) and before the duplicate/quota checks, so a wrong-length number is a 400 rather than a persisted owner.\n\n4. **`InvalidTelephoneException.java`** \u2014 javadoc updated to note the new rejection cause.\n\n**Behavior:** the E.164 country code selects the required national length \u2014 `+61` demands 9 national digits, `+1` demands 10 \u2014 and any mismatch flows through the existing `InvalidTelephoneHandler` to a 400. The new acceptance test (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, as does the full suite.\n\nNote: `RequireOwnerFields` binds `@RequestBody` without `@Valid`, so the generated DTO's OpenAPI `^[0-9]*$` pattern is not enforced at binding \u2014 the `+`/spaces in inputs like `+61 123` reach the normalizer as intended.",
